# The emoji shorthand
#
# Every emotion and the commonest events have a glyph, and a glyph is a *second
# spelling*, not a second language: it goes through the same alias table as
# `lonely` -> `loneliness`, so there is not one line of runtime that knows the
# difference.  `tests/test_emoji.py` asserts exactly that -- this program and its
# word-for-word transcription produce byte-identical worlds.
#
# What the glyphs buy is density.  A love triangle is three people and six
# feelings, and written this way the whole setup fits in the space the word
# `jealousy` takes on its own.  Run it with --emoji and the state comes back in
# the same alphabet it was written in:
#
#     catharsis run examples/emoji.feel --emoji
#
# 👤 agent   ❤️ love     🤝 trust    🕊️ hope      😄 joy       🦁 pride
# 🔍 curious 🙏 grateful 😠 anger    😨 fear      🤔 doubt     😢 sadness
# 🖤 grief   🙈 shame    😞 guilt    😒 envy      💚 jealousy  🧍 lonely
# 😲 surprise 🛤️ regret  🧊 resentment
# 💔 betray  🙇 apology  🤲 forgive  👁️ witness  🎁 gift      🧠 remember
# 💭 recall  🙅 deny     🧘 accept   🔀 choice    ⏱️ tick

👤 alice bob carol

❤️ alice bob 0.9
❤️ bob alice 0.7
🤝 bob alice 0.8

❤️ carol bob 0.8
💚 carol alice 0.7
🧍 carol 0.6

⏱️ 12

# Carol is not told to do anything about any of this.
observe
